//? Title: Chien Search: Actual Coefficient States over a Prime Field
//? Subtitle: Multiplicative order, complete root sets, shortcut paths and checked sweep limits
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Finding every root of a polynomial over a finite field requires covering the
field without missing candidates or overflowing modular arithmetic. Chien search
visits nonzero elements in primitive-root order and updates each coefficient's
contribution by a fixed multiplier. We inspect actual states in a small example,
retain the original GF(13) cubic and plot, and count the operations this native
implementation really performs. Canonical C++ now records generator rejections,
coefficient states and shortcut paths without replay, with explicit limits for
field sweeps and generator discovery. Prime fields, binary extension fields,
formal zero polynomials and repeated roots have different contracts; those
distinctions matter before using root search inside a decoder.
)

\toc

> GF(7): roots in discovery order, not numeric order

Take f(x)=x²+2x+6=(x−2)(x−3) over GF(7). The smallest primitive root
is g=3. It visits [1,3,2,6,4,5], covering every nonzero element once.
Starting with coefficient contributions [6,2,1], the fixed multipliers
[1,3,2] produce [6,6,2] at x=3, then [6,4,4] at x=2. Both sums are
zero modulo seven. The returned roots are therefore [3,2], rather than [2,3].

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/chien_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::chien_search_trace({6,2,1},7);
  if(trace.roots!=std::vector<std::int64_t>{3,2} || trace.steps.size()!=6)return 1;
  std::print("| exponent i | point g^i | actual terms | modular sum | root |\n| ---: | ---: | :--- | ---: | :--- |\n");
  for(const auto& s:trace.steps)std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",s.exponent,s.point,s.terms,s.value,s.root);
  std::print("\nNative root order: {}. After the closing update: point {}, terms {}.\n",trace.roots,trace.final_point,trace.final_terms);
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(55,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle node;node.width=5.1;node.height=2.6;node.font_size=20;
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<3;++i) {
    const auto& s=trace.steps[i];if(s.root)node.fill={220,252,231};
    const auto id=std::format("state{}",i);
    diagram.node(id,{7.5*i,0},std::format("i={}, x={}\nterms {}\nsum={} mod 7",s.exponent,s.point,s.terms,s.value),node);
    if(i)diagram.edge(std::format("state{}",i-1),id,"update terms");
  }
  diagram.text({7.5,2.7},std::format("GF(7), primitive generator {}: update terms, then sum",trace.generator),20)
      .text({7.5,-2.7},std::format("fixed multipliers {}; point advances by multiplication by {}",trace.multipliers,trace.generator),18)
      .save_svg("build/chien_method.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| exponent i | point g^i | actual terms | modular sum | root |
| ---: | ---: | :--- | ---: | :--- |
| 0 | 1 | [6, 2, 1] | 2 | false |
| 1 | 3 | [6, 6, 2] | 0 | true |
| 2 | 2 | [6, 4, 4] | 0 | true |
| 3 | 6 | [6, 5, 1] | 5 | false |
| 4 | 4 | [6, 1, 2] | 2 | false |
| 5 | 5 | [6, 3, 4] | 6 | false |

Native root order: [3, 2]. After the closing update: point 1, terms [6, 2, 1].
\image(figures/chien_method.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Three actual successive coefficient states; the first two roots occur at points three and two.)
\alttext(Three boxes show points one, three and two with term vectors [6,2,1], [6,6,2] and [6,4,4]. The latter two sum to zero modulo seven.)

For normalized coefficients c_j and point g^i, maintain

$$
t_j^{(i)}=c_jg^{ij},\qquad f(g^i)=\sum_jt_j^{(i)}\pmod p,
\qquad t_j^{(i+1)}=t_j^{(i)}g^j\pmod p.
$$
\alttext(Term j is coefficient j times g to the product i times j. Summing the terms gives the value; the next term multiplies by the fixed g to the j.)

At i=0, g^0=1, so the initial terms are the normalized coefficients.
Multiplying by g^j preserves the displayed invariant by induction. A primitive
generator has order p−1, so its p−1 powers are distinct and exhaust the
nonzero field. Checking c0=f(0) separately completes the field coverage.
The modular sum is therefore zero exactly at each returned candidate root.
This proves the recurrence and coverage, not the existence of an error-locator
polynomial or the correctness of a complete decoding pipeline.

> Preserve the GF(13) cubic and check its values in numeric order

The original polynomial is x³+2x²+3x+4 over GF(13). Keep all thirteen
values and the root x=2. The search visits powers of g=2; the table and plot
below use numeric x order for readability. Native poly_evaluate_gf() supplies
the separate Horner evaluation path, replacing the report's unchecked local
multiply/add helper. Independent unsigned-arithmetic oracles are used in the
native tests rather than treating two shared-library calls as independent proof.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/chien_search.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <set>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const dm::algorithms::GFPoly f{4,3,2,1};const std::int64_t p=13;
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::chien_search_trace(f,p);
  std::vector<double> xs,ys,rx,ry;std::vector<std::int64_t> horner_roots;
  std::set<std::int64_t> distinct;
  std::print("Polynomial: {}. Actual primitive generator: {}.\n\n",dm::algorithms::poly_string_gf(f,p),trace.generator);
  std::print("| x | native Horner f(x) | root |\n| ---: | ---: | :--- |\n");
  for(std::int64_t x=0;x<p;++x) {
    const auto value=dm::algorithms::poly_evaluate_gf(f,x,p);distinct.insert(value);
    xs.push_back(x);ys.push_back(value);if(!value)horner_roots.push_back(x);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n",x,value,value==0);
  }
  auto sorted=trace.roots;std::sort(sorted.begin(),sorted.end());if(sorted!=horner_roots)return 1;
  for(const auto root:trace.roots){rx.push_back(root);ry.push_back(0);}
  std::print("\nSorted roots: {}. Native Horner agrees: {}. Distinct observed values: {} of {}.\n",sorted,sorted==horner_roots,distinct.size(),p);
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.line(xs,ys,"values",{37,99,235},1.5).points(xs,ys,"values",{37,99,235},4)
      .points(rx,ry,"roots",{220,38,38},8).abline_h(0,{100,116,139},1)
      .legend({"f(x) mod 13","roots"},{{37,99,235},{220,38,38}})
      .title("Original GF(13) cubic: one root, nine values")
      .x_label("field element x").y_label("f(x) mod 13").x_limits(-0.5,12.5).y_limits(-0.7,14)
      .save_svg("build/chien_search.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Polynomial: x^3 + 2x^2 + 3x + 4. Actual primitive generator: 2.

| x | native Horner f(x) | root |
| ---: | ---: | :--- |
| 0 | 4 | false |
| 1 | 10 | false |
| 2 | 0 | true |
| 3 | 6 | false |
| 4 | 8 | false |
| 5 | 12 | false |
| 6 | 11 | false |
| 7 | 11 | false |
| 8 | 5 | false |
| 9 | 12 | false |
| 10 | 12 | false |
| 11 | 11 | false |
| 12 | 2 | false |

Sorted roots: [2]. Native Horner agrees: true. Distinct observed values: 9 of 13.
\image(figures/chien_search.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The original thirteen evaluations with their root highlighted; repeated outputs mean the polynomial is not a permutation.)
\alttext(A discrete value plot over zero through twelve has a single root at two and only nine distinct residue values.)

The old report said the polynomial takes every residue. It takes only nine
of the thirteen possible values, with repetitions. Visiting every input does
not imply producing every output. Connections between discrete plot points
guide the eye; they do not express continuity, interpolation or an
unpredictable random process over a finite field.

> A primitive root must pass its order tests; operation counts include the code actually run

For each distinct prime divisor q of p−1, a candidate generator must satisfy
g^((p−1)/q)≠1 modulo p. If its order were a proper divisor of p−1, at least
one such exponent would be a multiple of that order, causing rejection.
Passing every test therefore certifies full order. In GF(7), g=2 fails
2³=1 and cycles through only [1,2,4], missing both roots of the worked
quadratic. The next candidate, g=3, passes the two required tests.

The table records those actual tests. The degree sweep then counts the native
implementation's coefficient-state products. For degree at least two, it
updates all d+1 coefficient terms, including the constant, at every one of the
p−1 nonzero points. It also preserves the final unused update that closes the
orbit, advances the point, and computes each multiplier g^j separately.
Consequently, a degree-only count is not the complete executed operation count.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/chien_work.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto worked=dm::algorithms::chien_search_trace({6,2,1},7);
  std::print("| generator candidate | prime divisor q | actual power value | passes this test |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |\n");
  for(const auto& test:worked.generator_tests)std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",test.candidate,test.q,test.value,test.value!=1);
  std::print("\n| degree | branch | field checks | term products | point products | multiplier setup products |\n| ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<double> degrees,actual,degree_only;
  for(std::size_t degree=0;degree<=8;++degree) {
    const dm::algorithms::GFPoly f(degree+1,1);const auto trace=dm::algorithms::chien_search_trace(f,13);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",degree,trace.path==dm::algorithms::ChienPath::Incremental?"sweep":"shortcut",
        trace.work.field_points,trace.work.term_products,trace.work.point_products,trace.work.setup_products);
    degrees.push_back(degree);actual.push_back(trace.work.term_products);degree_only.push_back(12*degree);
  }
  const auto cubic=dm::algorithms::chien_search_trace({4,3,2,1},13);
  std::print("\nOriginal cubic: {} generator steps, {} generator products, {} setup products, {} term products, {} point products, {} sum additions.\n",
      cubic.work.generator_steps,cubic.work.generator_products,cubic.work.setup_products,cubic.work.term_products,cubic.work.point_products,cubic.work.sum_additions);
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.line(degrees,actual,"actual",{37,99,235},1.5).points(degrees,actual,"actual",{37,99,235},4)
      .line(degrees,degree_only,"formula",{234,88,12},1.5).points(degrees,degree_only,"formula",{234,88,12},4)
      .legend({"actual term updates","degree-only formula"},{{37,99,235},{234,88,12}})
      .title("GF(13): actual coefficient-state products")
      .x_label("normalized polynomial degree").y_label("modular products").y_limits(-2,135)
      .save_svg("build/chien_work.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| generator candidate | prime divisor q | actual power value | passes this test |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |
| 2 | 2 | 1 | false |
| 3 | 2 | 6 | true |
| 3 | 3 | 2 | true |

| degree | branch | field checks | term products | point products | multiplier setup products |
| ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | shortcut | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |
| 1 | shortcut | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |
| 2 | sweep | 13 | 36 | 12 | 3 |
| 3 | sweep | 13 | 48 | 12 | 6 |
| 4 | sweep | 13 | 60 | 12 | 9 |
| 5 | sweep | 13 | 72 | 12 | 13 |
| 6 | sweep | 13 | 84 | 12 | 17 |
| 7 | sweep | 13 | 96 | 12 | 22 |
| 8 | sweep | 13 | 108 | 12 | 26 |

Original cubic: 3 generator steps, 7 generator products, 6 setup products, 48 term products, 12 point products, 48 sum additions.
\image(figures/chien_work.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual coefficient-state products and the abstract 12·degree formula; the orange line is a formula, not a second measured implementation.)
\alttext(Actual products are zero for constant and linear shortcuts, then thirty-six at degree two and increase by twelve per degree. The degree-only formula lies twelve below the sweep counts.)

The original cubic performs 48 term updates, 12 point updates and six setup
products, plus seven products in generator tests. It also makes 48 modular
sum additions. Prime testing, normalization, allocations and integer operations
while factoring p−1 are outside those named counters. A generator step means
one trial-divisor attempt or one generator power test; repeated successful
divisions and products within a test are not extra generator steps.

The plot's shortcut discontinuity is deliberate: nonzero constants return no
roots, and linears use an inverse rather than sweeping the field. The orange
12d formula models one nonconstant-term update per point; the blue sweep
includes the constant term. Removing that identity multiply or the closing
update would change the counts. No elapsed times or speedup are claimed.
Fixed multipliers are useful to hardware architectures, but integer prime-field
modular products here are not binary-extension-field gate counts.

> Zero polynomials, multiplicities and field representations

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto zero=dm::algorithms::chien_search_trace({},13);
  const auto repeated=dm::algorithms::chien_search_trace({4,-4,1},13);
  dm::algorithms::GFPoly function_zero(14,0);function_zero[1]=-1;function_zero[13]=1;
  const auto all=dm::algorithms::chien_search_trace(function_zero,13);
  const auto binary=dm::algorithms::chien_search_trace({0,1,1},2);
  bool composite=false;try {(void)dm::algorithms::chien_search({},256);}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){composite=true;}
  if(zero.path!=dm::algorithms::ChienPath::Zero || all.roots.size()!=13 || !composite)return 1;
  std::print("Formal zero polynomial: legacy roots {}, recorded points {}.\n",zero.roots,zero.work.field_points);
  std::print("Repeated polynomial {}: distinct roots {}, squarefree={}.\n",dm::algorithms::poly_string_gf({4,-4,1},13),repeated.roots,
      dm::algorithms::poly_is_squarefree_gf({4,-4,1},13));
  std::print("Nonzero formal polynomial {}: {} distinct roots, all nonzero evaluations zero={}.\n",dm::algorithms::poly_string_gf(function_zero,13),all.roots.size(),
      std::all_of(all.steps.begin(),all.steps.end(),[](const auto& s){return s.value==0;}));
  std::print("GF(2) roots of x^2+x: {}; generator {}. Composite p=256 rejected: {}.\n",binary.roots,binary.generator,composite);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Formal zero polynomial: legacy roots [], recorded points 0.
Repeated polynomial x^2 + 9x + 4: distinct roots [2], squarefree=false.
Nonzero formal polynomial x^13 + 12x: 13 distinct roots, all nonzero evaluations zero=true.
GF(2) roots of x^2+x: [0, 1]; generator 1. Composite p=256 rejected: true.
// result_end

Mathematically the zero polynomial vanishes everywhere. The legacy API instead
returns an empty root vector for its normalized zero representation; the trace
marks that sentinel path explicitly. An empty result on that path does not mean
there are no mathematical roots. Nonzero constants have none, while a linear
polynomial has exactly one. The repeated quadratic (x−2)² returns root 2 once,
without its multiplicity. Root search is not squarefree factorization.

A nonzero formal polynomial of degree at least p can also vanish at every
field element: x^p−x is an example. This does not contradict the degree bound
on roots, since it has degree p and exactly p distinct roots. Its incremental
trace is different from the formal-zero sentinel.

The modulus here must be prime; arithmetic is ordinary residue arithmetic in
GF(p). A binary extension field GF(2^m), with m>1, requires a polynomial basis
and reduction polynomial (or an equivalent representation). Passing 256 as p
does not request GF(256). This report constructs no syndrome, error-locator
polynomial or inverse-power mapping from roots to codeword error positions.
It demonstrates the root-finding recurrence on prime-field examples.

> Complete-call limits and the direct-linear path

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  dm::algorithms::ChienOptions options;options.max_points=7;options.max_term_products=18;options.max_generator_steps=4;
  const auto exact=dm::algorithms::chien_search_trace({6,2,1},7,options);
  bool points=false,products=false,generator=false,huge=false;
  auto low=options;low.max_points=6;
  try {(void)dm::algorithms::chien_search_trace({6,2,1},7,low);}catch(const std::length_error&){points=true;}
  low=options;low.max_term_products=17;
  try {(void)dm::algorithms::chien_search_trace({6,2,1},7,low);}catch(const std::length_error&){products=true;}
  low=options;low.max_generator_steps=3;
  try {(void)dm::algorithms::chien_search_trace({6,2,1},7,low);}catch(const std::length_error&){generator=true;}
  dm::algorithms::ChienOptions bounded_generator;bounded_generator.max_generator_steps=1;
  try {(void)dm::algorithms::chien_search_trace({1,0,1},1000000007,bounded_generator);}catch(const std::length_error&){huge=true;}
  dm::algorithms::ChienOptions tiny;tiny.max_points=tiny.max_term_products=tiny.max_generator_steps=1;
  const auto linear=dm::algorithms::chien_search_trace({-1,1},9223372036854775783LL,tiny);
  if(!points || !products || !generator || !huge || linear.roots!=std::vector<std::int64_t>{1})return 1;
  std::print("Exact caps succeed: roots {}, {} field checks, {} term updates, {} generator steps.\n",exact.roots,exact.work.field_points,exact.work.term_products,exact.work.generator_steps);
  std::print("One-below caps reject: points={}, term products={}, generator steps={}.\n",points,products,generator);
  std::print("Billion-point trace rejected by generator cap before giant record reservation: {}.\n",huge);
  std::print("Near-INT64_MAX linear root {}, with {} field-point checks and {} generator steps.\n",linear.roots,linear.work.field_points,linear.work.generator_steps);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Exact caps succeed: roots [3, 2], 7 field checks, 18 term updates, 4 generator steps.
One-below caps reject: points=true, term products=true, generator steps=true.
Billion-point trace rejected by generator cap before giant record reservation: true.
Near-INT64_MAX linear root [1], with 0 field-point checks and 0 generator steps.
// result_end

Positive point and term-product caps are forecast from normalized degree and
checked before generator search or sweep allocation. Generator-step caps apply
to the actual search; trace-state reservation follows successful generator
discovery. Zero caps mean no explicit limit. All failed calls return no partial
root set or trace and leave input coefficients unchanged. Linear shortcuts use
inversion products, which are counted separately and do not consume sweep or
generator budgets. Unbounded high-degree searches remain intended for small p.

The nonzero sweep costs O((p−1)n) fixed-word operations for n coefficients,
with trial-factoring/generator discovery and multiplier setup in addition.
Ordinary calls retain O(n) state plus roots and factor-search metadata; tracing
adds (p−1)n coefficient values and generator-test records. These are not arbitrary-
precision bit-complexity or machine-time bounds. No observational dataset is
needed for exact field arithmetic and controlled conformance examples.

Native tests exhaust all 23,941 coefficient vectors through length five over
GF(2), GF(3), GF(5) and GF(7), including zero/trailing-zero representations.
An independent Horner oracle and a generator-order walk check values, coverage,
root order, every actual term state and operation counters. Another 1,000 signed
random polynomials and 1,000 near-limit linear cases test widened arithmetic.
Exact budgets, early huge-sweep rejection, malformed fields, retained closing
updates, copied ownership and the original polynomial/formatting cases remain
regression checks. AddressSanitizer and UBSan exercise the new cases.

> References

- R. T. Chien (1964). [Cyclic Decoding Procedures for Bose-Chaudhuri-Hocquenghem Codes](https://doi.org/10.1109/TIT.1964.1053699), IEEE Transactions on Information Theory 10(4), 357–363. The present implementation adapts the recurrence to prime-field integer residues.
- MathWorks. [gfroots: finite-field polynomial roots and representations](https://www.mathworks.com/help/comm/ref/gfroots.html). Its extension-field representation illustrates why a field characteristic and extension degree differ; it is not the API contract of this native prime-field implementation.
